---
/*
 * "Rules as Executed" position paper, rendered in full. Everything under
 * src/research/ is generated from the paper's LaTeX source by convert-html.py
 * (pandoc + citeproc; glossary first-use expansion, numbered sections), which
 * lives next to that source in the research repository: the body HTML, the
 * outline headings, and the meta JSON with title, authors, date and DOI that
 * this page, the research index and the landing tile all read. This page only
 * wraps that output in the site chrome. The typeset PDF is archived on Zenodo
 * under the DOI and is the authoritative version; the button links there.
 */
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import DocsOutline from '~/components/DocsOutline.astro';
import paperHtml from '~/research/rules-as-executed.html?raw';
import headings from '~/research/rules-as-executed.headings.json';
import meta from '~/research/rules-as-executed.meta.json';
import '~/styles/research.css';

const pathname = '/research/rules-as-executed';
const title = `${meta.title} · RegelRecht`;
const authors = meta.authors.join(' and ');
const description =
  `${meta.kind} by ${authors}: publish law execution as machine-executable ` +
  'specifications, so that decisions can be re-executed and rules analyzed.';
const doiUrl = `https://doi.org/${meta.doi}`;

// Every issue the "Discuss this passage" button (Base.astro) opens carries the
// contribution:paper label from the issue form, and names the paper in its
// Paper field. The label alone would list feedback on every paper; the quoted
// title narrows it to this one, and also catches issues filed by hand from the
// issue chooser as long as the paper is named. Open and closed both show:
// a closed thread is still part of the discussion of a frozen paper.
const discussionUrl =
  'https://github.com/MinBZK/regelrecht/issues?q=' +
  encodeURIComponent(
    `is:issue label:contribution:paper "${meta.title}" sort:updated-desc`
  );
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  kind="docs"
  pathname={pathname}
  title={title}
  description={description}
>
  <nldd-sidebar-section
    no-collapse
    sidebar-label="On this page"
    sm-padding-bottom="32"
    md-padding-bottom="64"
    lg-padding-bottom="96"
  >
    <nldd-title slot="header" size="1">
      <h1>{meta.title}</h1>
      <p slot="subtitle">{meta.subtitle}</p>
    </nldd-title>
    {/*
      The sidebar carries what belongs to the paper as a whole: where to get it,
      where to discuss it, and where you are in it.
    */}
    <nldd-container slot="sidebar" padding="16">
      <nldd-button-group>
        <nldd-button
          href={doiUrl}
          text="PDF on Zenodo"
          end-icon="external-link"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          variant="secondary"
        ></nldd-button>
        <nldd-button
          href={discussionUrl}
          text="Discussion on GitHub"
          start-icon="comment"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          variant="secondary"
        ></nldd-button>
      </nldd-button-group>
      <nldd-spacer size="8" />
      {/*
       * The selection affordance (Base.astro) is invisible until text is
       * selected, so nothing on the page says it exists; this line does.
       */}
      <nldd-text size="sm" color="secondary">
        Select a passage in the paper to start a discussion about it.
      </nldd-text>
      <nldd-spacer size="12" />
      <DocsOutline headings={headings} />
    </nldd-container>
    <nldd-rich-text centered>
      <p>
        <strong>{authors}</strong><br />
        <em>{meta.affiliation}</em><br />
        {
          meta.emails.map((email, i) => (
            <Fragment>
              {i > 0 && ' · '}
              <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
            </Fragment>
          ))
        }<br />
        {meta.kind}, {meta.date_display}<br />
        <a href={doiUrl}>doi:{meta.doi}</a>
      </p>
      <p><small>{meta.note}</small></p>
    </nldd-rich-text>
    <nldd-spacer size="24" />
    {/*
     * The paper is frozen (see AGENTS.md, "Published papers are frozen"); this
     * note is where its divergence from the current code is recorded instead.
     * It sits outside the data-discuss-paper rich-text on purpose: it is the
     * site's text, not the authors', and a selection in it is not a passage of
     * the paper. Add an entry when a later RFC changes something the paper
     * states as fact; never edit the paper to match.
     */}
    <nldd-rich-text centered>
      <h2 id="since-publication">Since publication</h2>
      <p>
        The paper is published as it was written on {meta.date_display}, when
        the reference implementation was on schema v0.5.6. The code has moved
        on since. Where the paper states something about the format or the
        engine that no longer holds, the list below says what changed and
        where it was decided. The paper itself is not edited.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Collections.</strong> Section 9.2 says the format has no
          aggregation over collections and announces bounded aggregation. That
          arrived in schema v0.5.7 as <code>FOREACH</code>, which iterates over
          a finite collection, filters it and can combine the results into a
          sum or a count (<a href="/rfcs/rfc-016">RFC-016</a>, see
          <a href="/concepts/collections">Collections</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The operation count.</strong> The 25 operations listed in
          section 9.2 are now 28: <code>FOREACH</code>, plus
          <code>DATE_PART</code> and <code>START_OF</code>, which read a year,
          month or day from a date and truncate a date to the start of a period
          (<a href="/rfcs/rfc-032">RFC-032</a>, schema v0.7.0). The
          <a href="/reference/schema">schema reference</a> keeps the version
          history the paper points to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Untranslatables are now markings.</strong> Section 5.4 and
          Figure 5 use a field <code>untranslatables</code> with
          <code>construct</code> and <code>suggestion</code>. Schema v0.7.0
          replaced it with <code>markings</code>, whose entries carry
          <code>about</code>, <code>reason</code>, <code>resolution</code>,
          <code>resolved_by</code> and <code>target</code>
          (<a href="/rfcs/rfc-031">RFC-031</a>, see
          <a href="/concepts/markings">Markings</a>). The <code>accepted</code>
          field works as the paper describes, and the engine still reads the old
          field in files on an older schema.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Discretion is an open term, not a gap.</strong> Section 5.4
          records a provision such as "naar het oordeel van de minister" as an
          untranslatable construct. Under RFC-031 such a provision is an open
          term whose <code>decided_per_case_by</code> names the authority that
          decides it per case; a marking is reserved for what the format cannot
          yet express (see
          <a href="/concepts/inversion-of-control">Inversion of Control</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Absent and unknown values.</strong> The paper does not
          distinguish a value that does not exist from one that nobody has
          supplied. The engine now does: an absent value (<code>null</code>) is
          a fact a law may test, while an unknown value propagates to the
          outcome with the names of the missing facts, so no decision rests on
          it (<a href="/rfcs/rfc-036">RFC-036</a>, schema v0.5.8).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Type checking.</strong> Section 5.4 names a mechanical schema
          check as what catches structural mismatches. A static type checker now
          runs after it, on validation and when the engine loads a law, and
          holds each expression to the types and nullability the law declares
          (<a href="/rfcs/rfc-037">RFC-037</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A second kind of departure.</strong> Section 4.6 says the
          format has one way of departing from a computed outcome: replacing the
          value. Schema v0.7.0 added a second, <code>voids: true</code>, for a
          provision saying that an entitlement does not arise at all, which is
          not the same as an entitlement of zero
          (<a href="/rfcs/rfc-027">RFC-027</a>, scoped by
          <a href="/rfcs/rfc-041">RFC-041</a>).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nldd-rich-text>
    <nldd-spacer size="24" />
    {/*
     * The paper body is a rich-text of its own, so it carries the whole of the
     * paper and nothing else: data-discuss-paper turns on the selection
     * affordance in Base.astro, where selecting text offers "Discuss this
     * passage" and the value names the paper in the issue title. A selection in
     * the byline above (authors, emails, DOI) does not offer it. Its headings,
     * paragraphs and figures are children of that grid, so the rhythm and the
     * column each one belongs in come from the design system.
     */}
    <nldd-rich-text centered data-discuss-paper={meta.title}>
      <Fragment set:html={paperHtml} />
    </nldd-rich-text>
  </nldd-sidebar-section>
</Base>
